<node id="11121178" title="Re^10: Split tab-separated file into separate files, based on column name (open on demand)" created="2020-08-29 00:51:26" updated="2020-08-29 00:51:26" _doctext_mode="strict"><type id="11">note</type>
<author id="830549">haukex</author>
<doctext field_name="doctext">&lt;blockquote&gt;&lt;i&gt;Did you try the code?&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;That's how I discovered the issue... &lt;c&gt;;-P&lt;/c&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;blockquote&gt;&lt;i&gt;I think the issue is that awk's arrays are not zero based.&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I checked, and older versions of &lt;c&gt;a2p&lt;/c&gt; did indeed set &lt;c&gt;$[ = 1;&lt;/c&gt; - apparently while having other strange bugs in the output, it looks like to me (at least on my system, the output is strangely chopped up, e.g. "&lt;c&gt;next linine;&lt;/c&gt;" or "&lt;c&gt;}tinue {&lt;/c&gt;"). After 5.10, &lt;c&gt;a2p&lt;/c&gt; dropped the &lt;c&gt;$[&lt;/c&gt; assignment, but added adjustment of array indicies in some places, while not adjusting the array indicies in other places, e.g. &lt;c&gt;for ($i = 1; $i &lt;= $#Fld; $i++) { $Fields[$i] = $Fld[$i]; }&lt;/c&gt; became &lt;c&gt;for ($i = 1; $i &lt;= ($#Fld+1); $i++) { $Fields[($i)-1] = $Fld[$i]; }&lt;/c&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
</doctext>
